Z||000000||sinoloa||10-29-1999||08:42 PM||||I'm glad I found this wonderful site. I'm almost ready to give up on my very cute little red fiero.  It's a 1988 4 cylindar.  I have replaced the IAC and the TPS sensors and  the mechanics in our area told me that they will not touch a fiero and are scared of them.  Anyway, mine will not idle properly. It cranks up and runs at 2500 rpm after 30 minutes driving, it shows normal.  A mechanic has told me that it has a cracked intake manifold or a blown intake gasket.  To restart the car it acts like it tries to flood out.  Anybody got any clues what this could be? Help would be appreciated. Not many fieros or fiero mechanics in this area.||||reg||8
Z||000001||theogre||10-30-1999||05:14 PM||||Is yours an automatic?<P>look for vacuum leaks. check the EGR over good. <P>Consider rebuilding the TBI. this is basically replacing all the gaskets and cleaning it. The gasket under the 700 TBI's is some type of aluminum with a nickle loaded sealant on top of it. It ends up leaking, especially if the air cleaner whacks the trunk wall wich is common with 87-88's. Make sure you use the right gaskets. the kit comes with several diferant ones. use the ones that have the same cutouts as the originals. The kit for the TBI is about 35$us.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||sinoloa||10-31-1999||02:05 PM||||yes, mine is an automatic. Thank you for the help!  I'll try that.  ||||reg||10
Z||000003||Boxdin||10-31-1999||10:56 PM||||I can't remember exactly why now, but our 87 4cyl coupe 5sp thought the choke was on, all the time. I t was rich enough to smell it. It also important too to have the hot coolant pipes clear to the intake manifold to heat the bottom of it.  If that heated process dosen't occur, it will lean sputter. ||||reg||1
